You are building phase 1 of this repository's hackathon entry. Jacob Schoenberg approved the spec in docs/spec.md and said "all looks good build" on 2026-11-05, in a separate setup session that launched you headless. First read CLAUDE.md, every file in .claude/rules/, docs/spec.md and docs/methodology/feasibility-2026-11-05.md. The rules in them are hard rules.

WORKING STYLE (required; the previous attempt, session 49412096, died after reading the files: its next reply was too large and timed out on all 9 retries)
- Write one file per tool call, each under about 200 lines. Split modules rather than writing one giant file, and never write several files in one reply.
- Keep replies short: one or two tool calls per step. Read files in ranges, grep for functions, and never re-read a file you already have.
- Commit after each module that passes its tests, so progress survives a crash.
- It is about 1:15 am on 2026-11-06 and the submission is later today. Aim to finish phase 1 in about 2 hours.

PHASE 1 GOAL: the world runs end to end with no AI. That means the world, seasons, hornets with evolution, and bees driven by scripted policies (random, rule, oracle) through the motor layer, with full logging, tests and calibration. Make no model calls in this phase; the spend is $0.

FIRST, within your first few commits: write docs/BUILD_NOTES.md with a "## Log format" section giving the exact files and field names of the run directory (layout file, per-tick state JSONL, events, per-season metrics). Commit and push it early, because a parallel session builds the viewer against it. Keep that format stable afterwards. If you must change it, update the section in the same commit.

DELIVERABLES
1. Modules under src/digital_life/. Suggested: world.py (layout generation, hive, entrances, bloom sites, seasons), hornets.py (genome, behaviour, evolution), bees.py (bee state, life, sting, heal, delivery, hatch, queen floor, senses), motor.py (known map, A*, explore toward a frontier, flee), policies.py (random, rule and oracle scripted policies), sim.py (tick loop, conditions, logging; entrypoint run(params, seed, log) for experiments/run.py), metrics.py.
   - The policy interface must let phase 2 plug in the AI mind without touching sim.py. A policy receives only a Perception object built from that bee's senses and memory, never the World, and returns a decision of the spec's shape (intent, sound, remember, say).
2. Configs in experiments/configurations/:
   - calibrate_*.toml;
   - demo_small.toml (about 40x24, one short year, rule bees);
   - full-size configs for each scripted condition (rule_bees, random_bees, oracle_bees).
3. Logging as in the spec's Logging section, through RunLog:
   - a compact per-tick state JSONL, the layout file, events, and per-season metrics;
   - a full 2,000-tick run directory under about 5 MB (gzip the state file if needed);
   - scratch files on /dev/shm.
4. Tests in tests/:
   - world generation is deterministic;
   - the hive has 3 entrances, and hornets never enter hive cells;
   - a sting halves life, stuns for 2 ticks, and respects the 10-tick cooldown;
   - heal happens once per life;
   - delivery doubles life, capped at 3L;
   - a bee hatches only below the cap;
   - the queen floor works;
   - winter drain rates are correct;
   - hornet evolution changes genes, and frozen_hornets does not;
   - the motor layer never uses cells the bee has not seen;
   - Perception never contains unseen bloom or hornet coordinates (the scripted-policy leak test; the AI-prompt leak test comes in phase 2);
   - the same seed gives an identical run.
5. Calibration with scripted bees, recorded in EXPERIMENTS.md under a "## Setup calibration" section. This is not an "## Experiment" section, and it is not a result.
   - Tune base life L, the minimum bloom distance and maze density, so that in spring with hornets present:
     - oracle bees deliver on most trips;
     - random bees almost never deliver;
     - rule bees deliver rarely.
   - Use 3 seeds per calibration point, and keep calibration under about 45 minutes of compute.
   - Report the numbers from the summary.json files, plus the final values.
6. `make demo` runs demo_small.toml with rule bees, with no key, in under a minute.
7. docs/BUILD_NOTES.md as a handoff for the later phases: module map, the policy interface, Perception fields, log formats, calibration results, known issues.

RULES FOR THIS SESSION
- Commit small and often with plain messages, including the agent_traces changes. Push with `.local/push.sh -q origin main` after each meaningful commit. Never rewrite history.
- Run `make test` before each commit that touches src/, experiments/ or tools/.
- Add short plain explanations to ARCHITECTURE.md as mechanisms land. Leave README, AI_DISCLOSURE and the experiment write-ups for later phases.
- No model calls. No GitHub. Never print secrets.
- The shared VM's disk is tight. Run `df -h /` before large writes, keep scratch on /dev/shm, and delete it when done.
- If part of the spec is impossible or clearly wrong, make the smallest sensible change. Record it in DECISIONS.md as an agent proposal awaiting Jacob, and in BUILD_NOTES.md.
- Stop when deliverables 1 to 7 are done and the tests pass. End with a short report: what is built, the calibration numbers, and open issues.

